1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,920
Push a heavy cupboard.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:02,820
Nothing.

3
00:00:02,577 --> 00:00:03,477
Push harder.

4
00:00:03,474 --> 00:00:04,463
Still nothing.

5
00:00:04,463 --> 00:00:06,932
Then, all at once, it jerks forward.

6
00:00:07,332 --> 00:00:10,516
Why does it stay stuck, and
then give way so suddenly?

7
00:00:10,966 --> 00:00:14,427
Diya pushes her grandmother's
steel trunk, to sweep under it.

8
00:00:14,777 --> 00:00:17,020
Starting it takes all her strength.

9
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,299
But once it slides, a
gentler push keeps it going.

10
00:00:20,299 --> 00:00:21,199
Why?

11
00:00:21,284 --> 00:00:23,578
The answer is two kinds of friction.

12
00:00:23,578 --> 00:00:24,999
Static, and kinetic.

13
00:00:25,599 --> 00:00:27,045
First, what is friction?

14
00:00:27,395 --> 00:00:31,933
When one surface tries to slide over
another, the surface pushes back along

15
00:00:31,933 --> 00:00:32,874
the contact.

16
00:00:32,874 --> 00:00:34,464
That force is friction.

17
00:00:34,864 --> 00:00:38,150
If the body is still at
rest, it is static friction.

18
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:40,962
It opposes the sliding
that is about to start.

19
00:00:41,362 --> 00:00:44,944
If the body is already sliding,
it is kinetic friction.

20
00:00:44,944 --> 00:00:47,588
It opposes the sliding that is going on.

21
00:00:48,238 --> 00:00:49,880
To the motion notebook.

22
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,922
Diya's trunk, forty kilograms.

23
00:00:51,922 --> 00:00:54,486
She pushes, slowly harder and harder.

24
00:00:54,836 --> 00:00:55,901
Forty newtons.

25
00:00:55,901 --> 00:00:57,384
The trunk stays put.

26
00:00:57,384 --> 00:01:00,599
So friction is forty
newtons too, pointing back.

27
00:01:00,999 --> 00:01:02,136
Eighty newtons.

28
00:01:02,136 --> 00:01:03,283
Still at rest.

29
00:01:03,283 --> 00:01:04,685
Friction is eighty.

30
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:06,675
It grows to match her push.

31
00:01:07,075 --> 00:01:08,972
One hundred and sixty newtons.

32
00:01:08,972 --> 00:01:11,590
That is the most static friction can give.

33
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:13,209
And the trunk gives way.

34
00:01:13,609 --> 00:01:16,651
At once, friction drops
to one hundred and twenty.

35
00:01:16,651 --> 00:01:20,823
For a moment the push beats it, and
the trunk speeds up, as Diya eases

36
00:01:20,823 --> 00:01:21,723
off.

37
00:01:21,681 --> 00:01:24,216
Now her push is one hundred and twenty.

38
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:28,303
Push and friction balance, so the
trunk slides at a steady speed.

39
00:01:28,703 --> 00:01:33,392
On the graph, friction climbs at forty
five degrees, as big as the push.

40
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:36,710
It peaks at one sixty,
then drops to a flat line.

41
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:38,229
Slow-mo replay.

42
00:01:38,229 --> 00:01:40,164
Freeze, as it gives way.

43
00:01:40,514 --> 00:01:42,593
Static friction is at its limit.

44
00:01:42,593 --> 00:01:46,854
Then friction falls to one twenty,
and the extra forty newtons speed the

45
00:01:46,854 --> 00:01:47,754
trunk up.

46
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:50,131
Now the rule, step by step.

47
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:51,381
Step one.

48
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,924
At rest, the net force is zero.

49
00:01:53,924 --> 00:01:55,404
So f s equals P.

50
00:01:56,154 --> 00:01:57,054
Step two.

51
00:01:56,995 --> 00:01:58,789
f s cannot grow forever.

52
00:01:58,789 --> 00:02:00,604
It is at most f s max.

53
00:02:01,354 --> 00:02:02,362
Step three.

54
00:02:02,362 --> 00:02:06,328
When P goes past f s max,
the body starts to slide.

55
00:02:07,078 --> 00:02:07,999
Step four.

56
00:02:07,999 --> 00:02:12,685
While sliding, f k is nearly
constant, and smaller than f s max.

57
00:02:13,435 --> 00:02:14,409
Step five.

58
00:02:14,409 --> 00:02:17,308
Then a equals P minus f k, over m.

59
00:02:18,058 --> 00:02:19,050
Remember this.

60
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:22,102
Static friction matches
the push, up to a limit.

61
00:02:22,102 --> 00:02:25,089
Kinetic friction is smaller,
and nearly constant.

62
00:02:25,789 --> 00:02:27,644
But why is starting harder?

63
00:02:27,644 --> 00:02:28,699
Link by link.

64
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:30,186
Zoom right in.

65
00:02:30,186 --> 00:02:32,661
Even smooth surfaces have tiny bumps.

66
00:02:32,661 --> 00:02:34,946
They touch only at a few points.

67
00:02:35,346 --> 00:02:39,218
At rest, these points press
together and stick, like tiny welds.

68
00:02:39,618 --> 00:02:42,566
To start sliding, all of
them must break at once.

69
00:02:42,966 --> 00:02:46,329
Once sliding, the points
get no time to stick firmly.

70
00:02:46,329 --> 00:02:48,291
So kinetic friction is smaller.

71
00:02:48,941 --> 00:02:49,943
Side by side.

72
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:54,032
Static: push a cupboard with fifty
newtons, and friction is fifty.

73
00:02:54,032 --> 00:02:56,461
Push with seventy, and it is seventy.

74
00:02:56,861 --> 00:03:01,120
Kinetic: a sliding trunk feels one
hundred and twenty newtons, whether

75
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,858
it slides slowly or fast.

76
00:03:03,458 --> 00:03:04,531
Now you try.

77
00:03:04,531 --> 00:03:06,741
A book lies on a level table.

78
00:03:06,741 --> 00:03:08,074
Nobody pushes it.

79
00:03:08,074 --> 00:03:10,336
How big is the friction on it?

80
00:03:10,336 --> 00:03:11,670
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:15,770
Zero!

82
00:03:15,437 --> 00:03:19,915
Nothing tries to slide the book,
so friction has nothing to oppose.

83
00:03:20,565 --> 00:03:21,693
One more case.

84
00:03:21,693 --> 00:03:25,137
The trunk slides at one
point two metres per second.

85
00:03:25,137 --> 00:03:26,525
Then Diya lets go.

86
00:03:26,925 --> 00:03:27,825
Your turn.

87
00:03:27,781 --> 00:03:31,570
While it slows down, is the
friction static, or kinetic?

88
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:32,830
Pause and decide.

89
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:36,930
Kinetic!

90
00:03:36,773 --> 00:03:38,333
It is still sliding.

91
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:42,101
One hundred and twenty newtons
act back until it stops.

92
00:03:42,101 --> 00:03:43,766
Then friction is zero.

93
00:03:44,416 --> 00:03:46,152
Now, one worked example.

94
00:03:46,502 --> 00:03:48,377
A twenty five kilogram box.

95
00:03:48,377 --> 00:03:53,025
Static friction can reach ninety
newtons, and kinetic friction is seventy.

96
00:03:53,025 --> 00:03:57,388
Find the friction and acceleration
for pushes of sixty, and one hundred

97
00:03:57,388 --> 00:03:58,288
newtons.

98
00:03:58,588 --> 00:04:00,580
Sixty is less than ninety.

99
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:03,907
The box stays at rest,
so friction is sixty.

100
00:04:03,907 --> 00:04:05,436
Acceleration, zero.

101
00:04:06,186 --> 00:04:08,023
One hundred beats ninety.

102
00:04:08,023 --> 00:04:10,895
The box slides, so friction is kinetic.

103
00:04:10,895 --> 00:04:12,130
Seventy newtons.

104
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:14,636
Net force, thirty newtons.

105
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:16,735
a equals thirty over twenty five.

106
00:04:16,735 --> 00:04:19,215
One point two metres per second squared.

107
00:04:19,865 --> 00:04:21,330
Now, the exam trap.

108
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:25,971
Many students say a resting box
always feels its maximum friction.

109
00:04:25,971 --> 00:04:28,488
So a sixty newton push meets ninety.

110
00:04:28,888 --> 00:04:31,153
But then the box would move backwards!

111
00:04:31,153 --> 00:04:34,415
Static friction only matches
the push, up to its limit.

112
00:04:35,065 --> 00:04:36,381
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:36,381 --> 00:04:40,295
In questions where the push changes,
and you find the friction at each

114
00:04:40,295 --> 00:04:41,195
stage.

115
00:04:41,434 --> 00:04:42,645
Exam level now.

116
00:04:42,645 --> 00:04:44,225
A ten kilogram block.

117
00:04:44,225 --> 00:04:48,904
Static friction can reach fifty
newtons, and kinetic friction is forty.

118
00:04:48,904 --> 00:04:52,300
A push of ten t newtons
starts at t equals zero.

119
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:56,723
Find the friction at three seconds,
and the acceleration at eight.

120
00:04:56,723 --> 00:04:57,985
Pause and solve.

121
00:05:03,385 --> 00:05:04,613
The answer is C.

122
00:05:04,613 --> 00:05:07,830
Thirty newtons, and four
metres per second squared.

123
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:11,314
At three seconds the push
is thirty, below fifty.

124
00:05:11,314 --> 00:05:13,332
At rest, so friction is thirty.

125
00:05:13,832 --> 00:05:17,576
At five seconds the push reaches
fifty, and the block slides.

126
00:05:18,076 --> 00:05:21,619
At eight seconds, eighty
minus forty is forty newtons.

127
00:05:21,619 --> 00:05:24,955
Over ten kilograms, four
metres per second squared.

128
00:05:25,555 --> 00:05:27,014
Here is your recap.

129
00:05:27,014 --> 00:05:28,514
Screenshot this card.

130
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:33,645
Can you answer these without help?

131
00:05:34,045 --> 00:05:34,945
One.

132
00:05:34,533 --> 00:05:37,986
Why is the trunk harder to
start than to keep sliding?

133
00:05:38,886 --> 00:05:39,786
Two.

134
00:05:39,376 --> 00:05:42,392
A box pushed with thirty
newtons does not move.

135
00:05:42,392 --> 00:05:43,871
What is the friction?

136
00:05:44,771 --> 00:05:45,671
Three.

137
00:05:45,358 --> 00:05:48,023
Why does the friction graph drop suddenly?

138
00:05:49,123 --> 00:05:52,905
Next time: put a second trunk
on top, and friction grows.

139
00:05:52,905 --> 00:05:53,888
By how much?

140
00:05:53,888 --> 00:05:56,190
One number, called mu, tells us.

141
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:57,995
The coefficient of friction.
